"\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = \\frac{6}{25}\\cos (5t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "596ac213-0811-5853-a6d8-87c46836aa72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228391 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d8042cd1-bac2-5d2b-b9a4-591acf3abc28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228392 #edb/ref 1080 "0.4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228392 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a68a0bc6-f30d-5411-b064-805f776486bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228393 #edb/ref 1080 "0.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bec5c4a2-e69a-58a3-94b3-3a681c2ec258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228394 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228394 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228394 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f269fd6-b9ae-5b17-b3d5-9362d69d8e71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228395 #edb/ref 1080 "0.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228395 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228395 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42cbcc13-0825-57af-be0a-c07b85f4c34d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228396 #edb/ref 1080 "0.15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228396 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228396 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67173ceb-8d72-5892-b1ac-cf5cbde74ac5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228927 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21759" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228927 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7e/7e54c9613c18a0ea26df6a4088598b566e532fba555dc08db0bcfd539b4c64d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "353e21f2-2d1d-55e3-ac86-19932d117997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228927 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228928 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228928 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/424cb52325c95f03708ed9ae5fe1d41e7b0d1c8bb8878e1dd8dc002aef214481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28fd19b3-762a-572e-9b52-27e17093ad93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228928 #edb/ref 1074 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50},$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{3}{100} & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{3}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}(3-{e}^{-10t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228929 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228929 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/96/962694e1706c47a4b060da6636e0b6e2f1653cb94137dca8dc59781cf74bd252.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3e7a060-f36d-5fd6-a6f6-5b321261fb0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228929 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229134 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c51535d3-6b6a-5df3-a41f-9bad8e2edfbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229135 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229136 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1045 "Modeling RC Circuits With First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186229986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229262 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b757aeb4-02cf-539c-8183-7dd7b2d63500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229691 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229691 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/726e1c886236a47159bf154fc1ad6384ffc4aa89c5b3cba6b0067a70a0a773f2.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "273c8997-da69-595e-88fa-e96806d37c3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-336594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1071 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9547864c-5c79-5eab-ae35-a6351dc3262f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229695 #edb/ref 1074 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{1}{12}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{1}{12}$ coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229702 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a4182416a97e5f0111b0823d6699f0ed39125f60289d7c7ebbb5f98d51d1a0f6.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0d9a933-a0e8-56f2-b25b-1f97723df869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229702 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229986 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229986 #edb/ref 1061 590193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229986 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c65bf754-eef0-5e84-bd00-35163477c0bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229986 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229987 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229987 #edb/ref 1061 590194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229987 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e6b2bca-9335-5322-8c53-df90532ee3d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229987 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229988 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229988 #edb/ref 1061 590195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229988 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10ceda14-e108-59d7-a5da-7c171a7f8f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229988 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229989 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229989 #edb/ref 1061 590198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229989 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40af9ba4-9866-5456-ae0c-24676110c40f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229989 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229990 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229990 #edb/ref 1061 590199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229990 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30306ff1-de14-5d7a-b50f-2c053569ee9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229990 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229991 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229991 #edb/ref 1061 590203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229991 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d11623bd-24d5-5e52-9186-276626749839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229991 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229992 #edb/ref 1061 590204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229992 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00e01a01-a199-5457-88d2-64f8282b622a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229992 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230203 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we've used differential equations to model quantities in circuits containing resistors and inductors. Now, we'll focus on circuits containing resistors and capacitors.\n\nA **capacitor** is a component of an electrical circuit that opposes *changes* in voltage across it by storing energy in an electric field.\n\n![](images/37/3729fc4786e8677a1a7bf20aa013d6c6943c6d69c73adc63dd743c3918e65db4.png)\n\nThe capacitance $C$ (measured in farads) of a capacitor determines how much charge $q$ (measured in coulombs) the capacitor stores for a given voltage, $V_C,$ according to the equation \n\n$$\nq = CV_C.\n$$\n\nAn **RC circuit** is an electrical circuit that contains both a resistor and a capacitor, most commonly in series with a voltage source. An example, with current $I(t),$ is shown below, consisting of a resistance $R,$ in ohms, a capacitance $C,$ in farads, and a source electromotive force (emf) $E,$ in volts.\n\n![](images/fe/feef8e5b715b6b0442dc27a65886d611b27d4d912842f992790e6309a78afda4.png)\n\nLet $q(t)$ denote the charge on the capacitor, measured in coulombs, as a function of time $t.$ Can we find a differential equation that best describes the charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor?\n\nFirst, note that since current is the rate at which electric charge flows, the circuit's current is the derivative of charge with respect to time: \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nNow, recall Kirchhoff's Voltage law, which states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops around the loop.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The resistor *opposes the flow of current*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_R$ proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R{:}$\n\n$$\nV_R = RI = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\n- The capacitor *opposes changes in voltage*, hence producing a voltage drop $V_C$ related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ So, \n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's Voltage Law, the source voltage $E$ is the sum of the voltage drops: $E = V_R + V_C.$\n\nAs a result, the basic equation governing the amount of charge on the capacitor $q$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\nE = R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{q}{C}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{1}{RC}q = \\dfrac{E}{R}.\n$$\n\nNote that since $I(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ solving for $q(t)$ also tells us the current in the circuit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230203 #edb/ref 1065 14571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230203 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "09f3c015-b628-5d46-bdb8-7bab430a5c0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230203 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230204 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\sin(2t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitance of $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$ Let's find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 40\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 40I = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n8\\sin(2t) = 40\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sin(2t).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230204 #edb/ref 1065 14573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230204 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f5a25a61-fffc-5324-8a7e-46c6697292bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230204 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230206 #edb/ref 1064 "In a first-order linear differential equation, the solution naturally splits into a transient term that decays over time and a steady-state term that remains as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nIn an RC circuit with a constant electromotive force (EMF), this steady-state value corresponds to the charge the capacitor approaches as $t \\to \\infty,$ regardless of the initial charge. Physically, this represents the maximum charge the capacitor can store under a constant applied voltage.\n\nTo demonstrate how to identify the steady-state charge directly from the general solution of an RC circuit model, suppose the charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 12q = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} +12q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-12t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 12\\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac{7}{120}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{7}{120}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-12t} + \\dfrac{7}{120}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = Ae^{-12t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac{7}{120}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge (the charge the capacitor approaches as time increases) is ${\\dfrac{7}{120}}$ coulombs." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230206 #edb/ref 1065 14587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230206 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea3cd500-dfe2-5980-bdd5-a70d81a1b019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230206 #edb/ref 1067 "Steady-State Charges for Constant Voltage RC Circuits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230207 #edb/ref 1064 "In a first-order linear RC circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient charge, which decays over time, and a steady-state charge, which oscillates with the same frequency as the input.\n\nAs time increases, the transient charge vanishes, leaving a steady-state charge that oscillates periodically and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state charge in the RC circuit below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc700238d93fd6529c7113cd1a650e8c03d796e606afd4b655123cd04ed11cc7.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\cos(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $\\dfrac{1}{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 50\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 50I = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t) = 50\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q = \\dfrac{2}{5}\\cos(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 3q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}(4t)) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}(4t) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta =\\frac{2}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{6}{125}$ and $\\beta = \\dfrac{8}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-3t} + \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t) \\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{6}{125}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{8}{125}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{6}{125}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{8}{125}\\right)^2} =0.08\\,\\textrm{coulombs}.\n$$\n\nThis amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the oscillating steady-state charge on the capacitor." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230207 #edb/ref 1065 14588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230207 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5d9cda5d-0b3c-5320-a70a-71d53eedf1aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230207 #edb/ref 1067 "Steady-State Charges for Sinusoidal Voltage RC Circuits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308910 #edb/ref 1080 "0.019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308910 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308910 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "157af9e8-0ff0-5ed6-8aed-241b398c65bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308980 #edb/ref 1080 "0.72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ea0ea24-6dfa-5652-8659-3fc0b2f343de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309108 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309108 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309108 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f6cd3ff-224c-593a-8d47-079eff9bf967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186